;; Seatbelt profile for the Open Half-Life media-parser worker on macOS.
;;
;; Rendered by `ohl-platform`'s macOS isolated-worker backend, which replaces
;; every `@IMAGE@` placeholder with the canonical, already-verified path of
;; the worker image, and handed to `/usr/bin/sandbox-exec -p` in the confined
;; child before it executes the image.
;;
;; Rules are last-match-wins, so the order below is load-bearing: a blanket
;; read, then the withdrawals, then the image re-allowed in case it lives
;; under a withdrawn tree.
(version 1)
(deny default)

;; The image itself: the one program this sandbox may ever execute.
(allow process-exec (literal "@IMAGE@"))

;; Reads are allowed broadly, and this is measured rather than chosen: under
;; `(deny default)` a hosted binary cannot reach `main` at all without
;; `file-read*`, because dyld must map the shared cache and libSystem before
;; any of our code runs. Enumerating those paths instead was tried and does
;; not survive contact with the platform — on Apple Silicon the cache lives
;; behind `/System/Volumes/Preboot/Cryptexes`, a firmlink whose resolved path
;; a `(subpath "/System")` rule does not match, and that location has already
;; moved once between macOS releases.
;;
;; What this costs is bounded by what the worker can do with a byte it reads.
;; It has no network, no writes, no `fork`, and no `exec` of anything but the
;; image, so its only channel out is the OWP/1 pipe the parent validates; and
;; the dispatcher opens nothing of its own — every payload byte it parses is
;; one the parent chose to answer inside a bounded window. The user's own
;; data is withdrawn below regardless.
(allow file-read*)
(allow file-map-executable)

;; Withdraw what actually matters: user data, mounted volumes, the root
;; account's home, and the system's own configuration.
(deny file-read* file-map-executable
    (subpath "/Users")
    (subpath "/Volumes")
    (subpath "/private/var/root")
    (subpath "/private/etc"))

;; ... but the image may itself sit under one of those trees (a developer
;; build, or a CI checkout under `/Users`), so it is re-allowed last.
(allow file-read* (literal "@IMAGE@"))
(allow file-map-executable (literal "@IMAGE@"))

;; Reading system configuration values. This is not optional and it is not
;; obvious: `std::rt::init` installs the main thread's stack guard page
;; before `main` runs, and sizing it queries the page size through sysctl.
;; Denied, that query fails, the guard-page `mmap` is handed a bogus size and
;; returns EINVAL, and the process aborts with "failed to allocate a guard
;; page: Invalid argument (os error 22)" — before any of our code exists.
;;
;; This was measured by attribution, not guessed. A C binary such as
;; `/usr/bin/true` never allocates a guard page and starts happily without
;; this rule, which made it a misleading thing to validate against; and the
;; failure looks like a Mach-exception-port problem, which it is not —
;; granting every Mach class changes nothing, and `sysctl-read` alone fixes
;; it. No Mach capability is granted here for exactly that reason.
(allow sysctl-read)

;; Restated for the reader; `(deny default)` above already covers them. The
;; worker has no business writing anywhere, reaching the network, creating a
;; process, or talking to a Mach service.
(deny file-write*)
(deny network*)
(deny process-fork)
(deny mach-lookup)
